# Markdown that runs

Not just pages — **full websites**. Routing, state, server calls, embeds, and reactive islands, all from plain Markdown files. No JavaScript or CSS required. Your docs and your code are all just text.

<a class="btn primary" href="/app/">See a whole app in one file</a>
<a class="btn" href="/docs/">Read the docs</a>
</section>

:note "Static pages ship zero JavaScript. The moment a page asks for reactivity, one ~2 KB runtime loads — never before. JS optional, CSS optional, always."

## A whole framework, in text

Everything a site needs, expressed as Markdown directives. No components, no config, no JSX, no special files:

- **Routing** — folders are routes. Drop a file in, it's a page. Prefix with `.` or `-` to hide it.
- **State & reactivity** — `:state`, `:button`, `:if`, and keyed `@loop`s patch the DOM directly. No virtual DOM.
- **Server calls** — `:fetch` pulls JSON into state; `:form action=` round-trips to any backend and degrades to a native POST without JS.
- **Composition** — `@include` embeds one Markdown file into another, passing values down Liquid-style.
- **Styling, optional** — colocate a `.skin` file for design tokens, or ship none and let the browser default.

